O|
M|                                     "THE SEARCHERS"
O|
M|                               Revised Final Screenplay by
O|
M|                                       Frank Nugent
O|
O|                
O|
T|               FADE IN
O|
M|               Behind the main title and the credits:
O|
S,G|               EXT. PLAINS COUNTRY - CLOSE SHOT - MOVING JUST ABOVE GROUND 
S,G|               LEVEL - A STUDY OF HOOFPRINTS - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N|               The hoofprints are deeply etched in the ground, picking their 
N|               way through scrubby desert growth. An occasional tumbleweed 
N|               drifts with the light breeze across the pattern of prints; 
N|               and lightly-blown soil and sand begin the work of erasing 
N,G|               them. The CAMERA FOLLOWING the hoofprints
O|
G|               RAISES SLOWLY TO:
O|
S,G|               EXT. PLAINS COUNTRY - LONG SHOT - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N,G|               We see the rider now. BACK TO CAMERA, jogging slowly along -- 
N|               heading down a long valley toward a still-distant ranch house 
N|               with its outlying barn and corrals.
O|
S,G|               EXT. PLAINS COUNTRY - MED. SHOT - MOVING - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N,G|               The CAMERA FRAMES and MOVES with the lone horseman. He is 
N,I|               ETHAN EDWARDS, a man as hard as the country he is crossing.
O|
N,I|               Ethan is in his forties, with a three-day stubble of beard.
O|
N|               Dust is caked in the lines of his face and powders his 
N|               clothing. He wears a long Confederate overcoat, torn at one 
N|               pocket, patched and clumsily stitched at the elbows.
O|
N|               His trousers are a faded blue with an off-color stripe down 
N|               the legs where once there had been the yellow stripes of the 
N|               Yankee cavalry. His saddle is Mexican and across it he carries 
N|               a folded serape in place of the Texas poncho...
O|
N,G|               Rider and horse have come a long way. The CAMERA HOLDS and 
N,G|               PANS the rider past and we see another detail; strapped onto 
N|               his saddle roll is a sabre and scabbard with a gray silk 
N|               sash wrapped around it... Horse and rider pass, moving closer 
N,I|               to the ranch as a little girl and a small dog come tearing 
N|               around the corner of the house.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE YARD OF THE EDWARDS RANCH - MED. SHOT - DEBBIE - 
S,G|               LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N|               She is staring wide-eyed at the distant horseman o.s.
O|
N|               Her little dog has seen him too and is barking excitedly.
O|
N,I|               DEBBIE quickly reaches to grab the dog by the scruff of the 
N,I|               neck, crouching over him. Debbie is 11 years old with a 
N,I|               piquant, memorable face.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE YARD - CLOSE SHOT - DEBBIE
O|
M|               Here we must establish and dramatize what it is about her 
M|               face that is memorable, so that if we were to see her again 
M|               five or six years later, we would know it is she -- perhaps 
M|               the eye color or the slant of eyebrow, or a trick of 
M|               scratching bridge of nose with crooked forefinger.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE EDWARDS HOUSE - MED. SHOT - AARON - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N|               The ranch house is of adobe, solidly built, with a sod and 
N|               cross-timbered roof, deep windows. A small gallery or porch 
N,I|               extends across the front. AARON EDWARDS comes through the 
N|               door, attracted by the dog's barking -- and then he, too, 
N|               sees the approaching horseman and comes farther out -- curious 
N,I|               but not at all apprehensive. Aaron is a lean, weathered and 
N,I|               tired man, with a down-swept mustache; a gentler-looking man 
N,I|               than Ethan and possibly a few years older.
O|
N,I|               As he squints off, studying the rider, his older daughter, 
N,I|               LUCY, comes out to stand behind him. Lucy is from 16 to 18 -- 
N,I|               a pleasant, feminine girl. She is carrying a mixing bowl 
N|               with some sort of batter in it, which she now completely 
N|               forgets to whip in her interest in the approaching stranger.
O|
N,I|               In the next instant MARTHA EDWARDS follows the daughter onto 
N,I|               the porch. Martha is a still-lovely woman, although the years 
N,I|               have etched fine wrinkles about her eyes and mouth, and work 
N,I|               has worn and coarsened her hands. Those hands will never be 
N|               idle when Martha is on scene... And now, while she shares 
N|               the family's interest in the approaching horseman, she 
N|               automatically notes that Lucy has forgotten her task -- and 
N|               she takes the mixing bowl from her and stirs the batter.
O|
S,G|               EXT. YARD OF THE EDWARDS HOUSE - FULL SHOT - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N,I|               Along the side of the house comes BEN EDWARDS, 14, with a 
N|               man-sized armload of chunkwood clutched to his chest. He, 
N|               too, has spotted the stranger and is all attention. So much 
N|               so that he trips, but recovers his footing. He pauses to 
N|               dump the wood into a woodbox by the door -- his eyes always 
N|               riveted on the oncoming rider -- and then he moves toward 
N|               the others, biting a splinter out of a finger. Beyond Ben, 
N,I|               MARTIN PAULEY emerges from the barn and crosses the open 
N,G,I|               ground heading toward CAMERA. Martin is somewhat under 20, a 
N,I|               lithe, perfectly coordinated male animal, with Indian-straight 
N,I|               hair and a white man's eyes. He is carrying bridle or other 
N|               horse-gear. He looks to the family on the porch -- to see if 
N|               they recognize the stranger -- then out again. He continues, 
N|               followed by Ben, toward where
O|
N|               Debbie crouches over her dog.
O|
S,G|               EXT. PLAINS COUNTRY - LONG SHOT - ETHAN - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N|               As he rides downslope toward the house.
O|
M|               THE CREDITS END.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE EDWARDS RANCH - MED. CLOSE SHOT - MARTHA, LUCY, AND 
N|               AARON - LATE AFTERNOON
O|
N|               Suddenly, Martha's eyes widen as she -- even before Aaron -- 
N|               recognizes the distant rider. Her hand goes to her mouth to 
N|               check the name that trembles on her lips... An instant later 
N|               Aaron, too, identifies the oncoming horseman.
O|
C|                                     AARON
P|                              (incredulous)
U|                         Ethan?
O|
N|               He looks at her, frowning, then slowly steps out onto the 
N|               hard ground. Martha hands the bowl back to Lucy and follows 
N|               Aaron.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE YARD OF THE EDWARDS HOUSE - FULL SHOT - THE GROUP
O|
N|               as Ethan rides in and sits his horse, looking down at them.  
N|               There is a noticeable constraint on all of them. Finally:
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Hello, Aaron...
O|
N|               His eyes shift to Martha and hold. Ethan is, and always has 
N|               been, in love with his brother's wife and she with him.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Martha...
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
P|                              (a bit shakily)
U|                         Hello, Ethan.
O|
N|               Ethan slowly, stiffly swings out of the saddle. Aaron and 
N|               Martha exchange quick glances... troubled, puzzled. Aaron 
N|               pastes on an uncertain smile as Ethan comes around his horse 
N|               toward their side.
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         How's California?
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         How should I know?
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         But Mose Harper said...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         That old goat still creakin' 
U|                         around?... Whyn't someone bury him?
O|
N|               He goes to his saddle pack, begins unlacing it. Ben and Debbie 
N|               have inched closer -- half-shy, half-curious.
O|
N|               Debbie's dog begins sniffing at his heels. Ethan looks down 
N|               at them - not unfriendly, just a man not used to children.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Ben, ain't you?
O|
N|               Ben nods.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (frowning at Debbie)
U|                         Lucy, you ain't much bigger than 
U|                         when I saw you last.
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
U|                         I'm Deborah!
P|                              (pointing)
U|                         She's Lucy.
O|
N|               Ethan looks in the direction of the pointing finger.
O|
S,G|               EXT. YARD - ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               as Lucy steps down from the porch and approaches.
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         Lucy's going on seventeen now...
O|
C|                                     BEN
U|                         An' she's got a beau! Kisses him, 
U|                         too!
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         That's enough... Go on inside and 
U|                         help Lucy set the table... You, too, 
U|                         Deborah!
O|
S,G|               EXT. YARD - FULL SHOT - ANOTHER ANGLE
O|
N|               as Martin -- with slightly averted face -- crosses to take 
N|               the bridle of Ethan's horse and lead him away.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (wheeling on him)
U|                         MOMENTO!
O|
N|               Martin checks his stride, stares in surprise.
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
P|                              (contritely)
U|                         Martin!... Here we've been standing... 
U|                         Ethan, you haven't forgotten Martin?
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Oh... Mistook you for a half-breed.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (levelly)
U|                         Not quite... Quarter Cherokee. The 
U|                         rest is Welsh... So they tell me.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         You've done a lot of growin'...
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         It was Ethan found you squallin' in 
U|                         a sage clump after your folks was 
U|                         massacred...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (bluntly)
U|                         It just happened to be me... No need 
U|                         to make any more of it...
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         I'll take care of your horse for 
U|                         you, Uncle Ethan.
O|
N|               Again, he starts to lead away.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Hold on!
O|
N|               Martin stops again.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         I'll take this...
O|
N|               He completes unlacing the pack and takes it -- treating it 
N|               as though it contained something of value. Martin watches 
N|               with a touch of resentment: Ethan doesn't trust him.
O|
N|               Ethan turns and sees the look. He doesn't care what Martin 
N|               thinks, nor does he explain. Martin leads the horse off.
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         Supper'll be ready by the time you 
U|                         wash up... Let me take your coat for 
U|                         you, Ethan.
O|
N|               He hesitates, then grudgingly surrenders it -- conscious of 
N|               its sorry condition.
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
P|                              (smiling faintly)
U|                         And... welcome home.
O|
N|               He just nods, then turns to follow Aaron around the side of 
N|               the house toward the wash-up.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE EDWARDS HOUSE - CLOSE SHOT - MARTHA
O|
N|               She stands alone, looking after Ethan -- his coat in her 
N|               arms. She holds it against her breast for just a moment and 
N|               her eyes are tender.
O|
T|                                                               DISSOLVE TO:
O|
S,G|               INT. EDWARDS HOUSE - FULL SHOT - NIGHT
O|
N|               The family is finishing dinner -- and the scene is not quite, 
N|               but almost, a still-life. Loud in the room is the pendulum 
N|               tick of a Seth-Thomas clock on the mantel above the fireplace -- 
N|               in which logs are burning briskly. Ben crouches near the 
N|               fireplace, fascinatedly examining the scabbard and sabre 
N|               Ethan has brought home from the wars.
O|
N|               He tries to ease the blade just a bit out of its scabbard.
O|
N|               Aaron sits at one end of the hand-hewn table, Martha at the 
N|               other. At her right is Ethan, his fork scraping the last 
N|               crumb off his plate. Lucy sits at her father's right and 
N|               Martin at his left. Next to Martin is Debbie. In the center 
N|               of the table is the sorry remnant of what was once a meal. 
N|               Lucy and Martin have finished eating. Aaron is sipping his 
N|               coffee, and Martha -- her own plate largely untasted -- is 
N|               watching Ethan.
O|
N|               Ethan has shaved, changed his shirt. He straightens 
N|               contentedly and every eye is on him, expectantly.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Good.
O|
N|               The clock rattles alarmingly -- the usual preliminary to its 
N|               striking; and then it bangs out the strokes like a fire-alarm 
N|               gong. Eight fast clangs.
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         Ben! Deborah! Bed!
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
U|                         But I've got to help with the dishes.
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         Not tonight... Ben, put that sword 
U|                         back.
O|
C|                                     BEN
U|                         It's not a sword, ma... it's a saber!
P|                              (moving to Ethan)
U|                         Did you kill many damYankees with 
U|                         this sabre, Uncle Ethan?
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (matter-of-factly)
U|                         Some...
O|
C|                                     BEN
U|                         How many damYankees, Uncle Ethan?
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         Ben!... Martin, he'll sleep in the 
U|                         bunkhouse with you tonight.
O|
N|               Martin nods and crosses to kiss Martha good night.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         Good night, Aunt Martha... Uncle 
U|                         Aaron...
P|                              (he hesitates)
U|                         Good night, Uncle Ethan.
O|
N|               Ethan doesn't like being called Uncle -- as we must know 
N|               from the quick look he shoots at Martin. But he acknowledges 
N|               it.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Night.
O|
N|               Ben reluctantly puts the scabbard away, turns to Ethan.
O|
C|                                     BEN
U|                         Will you tell me tomorrow about the 
U|                         war?
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         The war ended three years ago, boy!
O|
C|                                     BEN
U|                         It did?... Then whyn't you come home
U|                         before now?
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         BEN!... Go 'long with Martin. MARCH!
O|
N|               As Ben reluctantly heads out with Martin, Deborah crosses to 
N|               Ethan's side and studies him gravely.
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
U|                         Lucy's wearing the gold locket you 
U|                         gave her when she was a little girl...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Oh?
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
U|                         She don't wear it much account of it 
U|                         makes her neck green.
O|
C|                                     LUCY
P|                              (aghast)
U|                         Deborah!
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
P|                              (defensively)
U|                         Well, it does... But I wouldn't care 
U|                         if you gave me a gold locket if it 
U|                         made my neck green or not.
O|
N|               Ethan looks at her gravely.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         'Fraid I...
P|                              (then he remembers 
P|                              something, rises)
U|                         Wait.
O|
N|               He crosses to where his pack is -- a side table or something -- 
N|               and burrows into it. Debbie is at his side.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         How about this?
O|
N|               It is a gold medal or medallion -- something appropriate to 
N|               Maximilian of Mexico -- suspended by a long multi-colored 
N|               satin ribbon.
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
U|                         Oh! LOOK! My gold locket!
O|
N|               She holds it high for mother -- and all -- to see. Martha 
N|               takes it and reacts at its weight.
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
U|                         It's solid gold... Ethan, I don't 
U|                         think she's old enough...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Let her keep it... Just something I 
U|                         picked up in Mexico.
O|
N|               Martha reluctantly surrenders it to Debbie's eager hand.
O|
N|               Aaron hasn't missed the word "Mexico" and looks sharply at 
N|               Ethan.
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
U|                         Oh, thank you, Uncle Ethan...
O|
C|                                     LUCY
P|                              (to Debbie)
U|                         Come along...
O|
N|               The two girls leave the main room. Martha and Aaron both 
N|               look at Ethan -- half expecting some further explanation.
O|
N|               He turns from them and looks into the fire. Martha begins to 
N|               clear the table. Aaron gets up, takes a pipe and a spill -- 
N|               lights it at the fire.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Passed the Todd place comin' in...
U|                         What happened to 'em?
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         They gave up... went back to the 
U|                         cotton rows... So'd the Jamisons...
U|                         Without Martha, I don't know... She 
U|                         wouldn't let a man quit.
O|
N|               Ethan turns and looks at her -- still busy with her dishes.
O|
C|                                     AARON
P|                              (change of tone)
U|                         Ethan, I could see it in you before 
U|                         the war...
P|                              (Ethan looks at him)
U|                         You wanted to clear out!
O|
N|               Martha freezes in what she's doing -- listening.
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         And you stayed out beyond all need 
U|                         to... WHY?
O|
N|               Ethan can't answer, but he takes it as a challenge and almost 
N|               welcomes it.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (hard)
U|                         You askin' me to clear out now?
O|
C|                                     AARON
P|                              (straightening -- 
P|                              with grave dignity)
U|                         You're my brother... You're welcome 
U|                         to stay as long as you got a mind 
U|                         to... Ain't that so, Martha?
O|
C|                                     MARTHA
P|                              (almost a whisper)
U|                         Of course he is.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         I expect to pay my own way...
O|
N|               Martha resumes her activity. Ethan crosses to his pack, 
N|               reaches into it for a leather pouch, brings it back and tosses 
N|               it onto the table. It lands with a resonant clink.
O|
N|               Both Martha and Aaron draw close to the table.
O|
M|               (NOTE TO WINTON HOCH: This scene should be dramatically back-
M|               lighted.)
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         There's sixty double eagles in 
U|                         there... twelve-hundred dollars.
O|
N|               He opens a waistline shirt button and hauls out a leather 
N|               money belt and drops that on the table.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         An' twice that in here.
O|
N|               He reaches into the belt and takes out a few mint-fresh gold 
N|               pieces which he slides across the table.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         ...only these got the late Emperor 
U|                         Maximilian's picture on 'em.
O|
N|               Martha picks up one of the gold pieces, staring at the face 
N|               on the coin: the same as that on the medal -- staring sharply 
N|               then at Ethan. Aaron is examining another coin with a 
N|               different interest.
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         Mint fresh... not a mark on 'em.
O|
N|               He glances questioningly at Ethan.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         So?
O|
N|               Aaron shrugs and crosses to a barrel chair. He raises the 
N|               seat and lifts out a pair of old boots, some rags of clothing 
N|               and then raises a false-bottom lid and drops pouch and money 
N|               belt into it. Carefully he replaces everything. During this 
N|               Ethan's attention has gone to Martha's hand, to one cut 
N|               finger, its wound barely healed. He takes the hand -- gently.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Cut yourself?
O|
N|               She nods and withdraws the hand.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (softly)
U|                         You were always hurting about your 
U|                         hands.
O|
N|               She looks quickly at him and self-consciously tries to hide 
N|               her hands, conscious of their work-worn appearance.
O|
N|               Then for a moment their eyes meet and hold -- and a world of 
N|               sadness and hopelessness is in the look.
O|
N|               Aaron closes the seat of the barrel chair.
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         Time for bed...
O|
N|               He picks up one of the lamps and starts away toward their 
N|               bedroom door. Martha looks at Ethan again. His expression is 
N|               bitter.
O|
C|                                     AARON
U|                         Night, Ethan... Come 'long, Martha.
O|
N|               She turns obediently and follows Aaron. Ethan looks after 
N|               them and waits as Aaron opens the bedroom door. Martha goes 
N|               into it and Aaron follows and closes the door.
O|
N|               Ethan crosses to the lamp on the mantel, blows it out.
O|
N|               Only the firelight strikes his face as he stares broodingly 
N|               at the closed bedroom door.
O|
