O|
D|               OMITTED
O|
S,G|               EXT. YARD OF THE EDWARDS' HOUSE - FAINT DAWN LIGHT
O|
N,I|               Debbie's dog is barking excitedly as six horsemen slowly 
N|               ride toward the house and dismount. A lamp goes on inside.
O|
N|               THE SIX HORSEMEN ARE:
O|
N,I|               CAPTAIN, THE REVEREND SAM CLAYTON, a big man with frosty 
N,I|               blue eyes, graying hair, a bristly full mustache and the air 
N,I|               of grave and resolute authority. He is a minister of the 
N|               Gospel with a .44 on his hip.
O|
N,I|               LARS JORGENSEN, the Edwards' neighbor, is a harried little 
N,I|               man, Scandinavian. As we shall find out soon, he has a brisk 
N,I|               and buxom wife and a rather astonishing brood of children.
O|
N,I|               BRAD JORGENSEN is one of these: sandy-haired, brash, amiable, 
N,I|               impulsive. He is in his early twenties.
O|
N,I|               CHARLIE MacCORRY, slightly older than Brad, is Sergeant of 
N,I|               Company A of the Rangers. (He is also Company A.) Charlie is 
N,I|               a taciturn, gently-spoken, competent man, clearly patterned 
N,I|               by his association with Captain, the Rev. Sam.
O|
N,I|               MOSE HARPER is an old scout -- a walking bone-rack, yet 
N,I|               capable of tireless feats of endurance. Some think him 
N|               "tetched" yet he has managed to endure to his age during a 
N|               time and in a region where few men lived to see their 
N|               grandchildren. He wears a ragged dark overcoat in all weather, 
N|               a narrow-brimmed hat with a feather in its band.
O|
N,I|               ED NESBY is a rancher and homesteader in his mid-thirties; 
N,I|               resolute, honest, self-effacing; nothing picturesque or 
N,I|               dramatic about him; just a solid citizen and a realist.
O|
O|               16-A
O|
S,G|               INT. EDWARDS' HOME - CLOSE SHOT - MARTHA
O|
N|               She is at the window of her bedroom, wrapper clutched with 
N|               one hand, lamp upraised in the other as she stares into the 
N|               dawn to see who these callers are. We hear the heavy foot- 
N|               falls of the approaching men, then a loud knock thrice 
N|               repeated -- an ominous sound.
O|
D|               OMITTED
O|
O|               17-A
O|
