S,G|               EXT. NEAR RIVER - PANNING SHOT - MORNING
O|
N,G|               The CAMERA SLOWLY PANS from a sun-touched butte or crag to 
N|               the file of men slowly walking their horses. An occasional 
N|               shred of mist drifts by. Everything about the little cavalcade 
N|               bespeaks tension, watchfulness. Suddenly -- and every man 
N,G|               sees it at the same time -- we see a file of eight Comanches 
N|               ride slowly out of a canyon at a distance, walking their 
N|               horses at the same pace and on a course roughly parallel 
N|               with, but slightly converging on, our group.
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
P|                              (softly)
U|                         Keep goin'...
O|
N|               Brad, who has been looking up ahead, sounds a new warning.
O|
C|                                     BRAD
P|                              (tensely)
U|                         Look!
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
U|                         Easy!
O|
O|               74-C
O|
S,G|               EXT. CANYON COUNTRY - LONG SHOT - PAST THE SEARCHERS
O|
N,I|               Another Indian file of eight angles out of a different canyon 
N|               and begins to cut in toward the group -- riding slowly, very 
N|               quietly. Clayton slightly alters course, veering slightly 
N|               away from the converging files, but still riding slowly. And 
N|               then, from ahead but at a 100 yards, another Comanche group 
N|               seems to rise out of the ground and slowly begins closing 
N|               the gap.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (to Clayton)
U|                         If you were tryin' to surround 'em, 
U|                         you sure succeeded.
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
U|                         How far's the river from here, Mose?
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         I been baptized, Reverend... yes 
U|                         suh, been baptized, thank ye...
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
U|                         Well, you better brace yourself for 
U|                         another one... Ya-HEE!
O|
N|               And with that yell, he drives spurs and cuts sharply at an 
N|               angle to the converging Indian files -- and every man is 
N|               with him. In the next instant, the Comanches whoop and give 
N|               chase.
O|
O|               74-D
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - FULL SHOT - THE CHASE
O|
N|               with the Ranger group short-cutting in such a way as to 
N|               outstrip the Comanche horsemen in a mad dash for the river.
O|
O|               74-E
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE RIVER'S EDGE - FULL SHOT - THE GROUP
O|
N|               Clayton flings his hand up in a signal to halt as the Rangers 
N|               reach the bank. They rein in, wheel their horses and are 
N,G|               reaching for the rifles as the Comanche vanguard races into 
N,G|               view -- to find themselves opposed by seven veterans, sitting 
N|               their horses, rifles at their shoulders.
O|
N|               The charge breaks as the seven rifles bark, almost in unison -- 
N|               and the Indians wheel to shelter.
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
U|                         YA-HEE!
O|
N|               And once again he spins his mount and takes off, across the 
N|               river, followed by the others.
O|
O|               74-F
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE RIVER - FULL SHOT
O|
N|               As the men pound across.
O|
O|               74-G
O|
