N|               The time is spring. It is a year and a half later.
O|
N|               The Jorgensen house is larger than the Edwards place -- of 
N|               sod and logs, with a covered breezeway connecting the two 
N|               separate buildings of the house: one being the keeping room, 
N|               the other the sleeping quarters of the numerous Jorgensen 
N|               brood. A meadowlark breaks into his sudden song. A dog or 
N|               two come barking around the side of the house as Ethan and 
N,G|               Martin ride slowly from behind CAMERA toward the house. In 
N|               that instant a lamp is lighted within the house and Lars 
N|               Jorgensen comes to the door.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE JORGENSEN HOUSE - FULL SHOT - NEAR DOOR - TWILIGHT
O|
N|               Jorgensen peers at the two men as they ride up - recognizing 
N|               them, of course, but ill-prepared for the change in their 
N|               appearance and full of unspoken questions.
O|
N|               Bearing a lamp, Mrs. Jorgensen hurries out to stand beside 
N|               her husband -- and her face works and tears begin to well in 
N,I|               her eyes. Two tow-headed boys -- 13 or 14 -- come after her. 
N|               Jorgensen makes a little signal with one hand, not even 
N|               looking at the boys, and they hurry out to take the reins as 
N|               Ethan and Martin dismount.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE JORGENSEN HOUSE - MED. SHOT - ETHAN AND MARTIN
O|
N|               The passage of time has stamped Martin -- and will continue 
N|               during our story more and more to stamp him -- in the image 
N|               of Ethan. Now it may show only in the set of his hat or trick 
N|               of standing; later it will be in his walk, in his speech (or 
N|               paucity of speech). Neither man is sure of his reception. 
N|               They are thinking of Brad -- dead because of their search; 
N|               and Martin is thinking of Laurie. And then Mrs. Jorgensen is 
N|               running across to Martin and has him in her arms as though 
N|               he were her son -- saying nothing, just holding him. He stands 
N|               frozen a moment and then he returns the embrace. Ethan watches 
N|               a moment, then crosses to Jorgensen.
