O|
N|               Laurie has reached the last page of the letter, reading it 
N|               by the lamp on the table. Jorgensen is knocking out the dead 
N|               ashes of his pipe.  Charlie is in the shadows, a guitar in 
N|               his hands -- not playing it, but occasionally softly picking 
N|               a note or chord. Mrs. Jorgensen is dabbing moist eyes with a 
N|               corner of her apron.
O|
C|                                     LAURIE
U|                         ...so we're setting out for New Mexico 
U|                         Territory in the morning... I am 
U|                         sorry I won't be back for Christmas 
U|                         again this year...
O|
N|               She swallows hard, pauses a moment in her reading.
O|
C|                                     MRS. JORGENSEN
P|                              (quick sympathy)
U|                         And you knittin' that muffler...
O|
C|                                     LAURIE
P|                              (impatiently)
U|                         What's the difference!
O|
C|                                     MRS. JORGENSEN
U|                         Well, I just thought it would be a 
U|                         sin and a shame not to let SOMEONE 
U|                         get some good of it...
O|
N|               She looks almost too obviously at Charlie, which annoys 
N|               Laurie. Laurie resumes her letter reading:
O|
C|                                     LAURIE
P|                              (peering closely)
U|                         There's a word crossed out... It 
U|                         looks like 'I wish' or 'I will'...
P|                              (she gives up)
U|                         Anyway... 'I set pen aside in the 
U|                         hope you are enjoying good health 
U|                         and your folks the same... I remain, 
U|                         respectfully...
P|                              (forlornly)
U|                         yours truly, Martin Paulie.'
O|
N|               That's all there is. Not a cross on it. Laurie just looks at 
N|               it. Jorgensen stands, pocketing his pipe, easing the crick 
N|               in his back. He ceremoniously removes the spectacles and 
N|               replaces them on the mantel.
O|
C|                                     JORGENSEN
U|                         They never find that girl.
O|
C|                                     LAURIE
P|                              (half to herself)
U|                         Yours truly...
P|                              (hotly)
U|                         And he even has to write his full 
U|                         name... Martin Pauley... not even 
U|                         just Martie!...
P|                              (she stands)
U|                         I don't care if he never comes back!
O|
N|               She heads for the front door.
O|
C|                                     MRS. JORGENSEN
P|                              (rising -- saying it 
P|                              without conviction)
U|                         Now, Laurie!...
O|
N|               Charlie hits the guitar a little stronger. Mrs. Jorgensen 
N|               looks at him -- and the matchmaker is at work.
O|
C|                                     MRS. JORGENSEN
U|                         Charlie, you'll stay for supper?...  
U|                         Now I won't take no for an answer.
O|
C|                                     CHARLIE
U|                         Thought of saying 'no' never crossed 
U|                         my mind, Miz Jorgensen... No place 
U|                         I'd rather be than right here, right 
U|                         now.
O|
N|               Mrs. Jorgensen smiles and moves about her duties. Laurie has 
N|               opened the door and is staring out wistfully... and Charlie 
N|               begins playing and singing a verse from "Skip to My Lou."
O|
C|                                     CHARLIE
P|                              (singing)
U|                         One old boot and a button shoe One 
U|                         old boot and a button shoe...
O|
T|                                                                   FADE OUT
O|
T|               FADE IN
O|
S,G|               EXT. DESERT COUNTRY - WIDE ANGLE - ETHAN AND MARTIN RIDING - 
S|               DAY
O|
N|               The search theme is heard again as the two riders, with single 
N|               pack horse, are heading south through New Mexico.
O|
N|               It is hot country.
O|
S,G|               EXT. DESERT COUNTRY - CLOSE MOVING SHOT - ETHAN AND MARTIN -
S|               DAY
O|
N|               Their faces are sun-tanned, burned dark and dry. Gone are 
N|               the heavy coats and clothing of their northern days. They do 
N|               not speak, just ride -- and there is the same bleak, fanatic, 
N|               hard look about them both. The music theme segues into 
N|               something livelier and Mexican as we -
O|
T|                                                               DISSOLVE TO:
O|
S,G|               EXT. A MEXICAN VILLAGE - FULL SHOT - ETHAN AND MARTIN - DAY
O|
N|               It is a small place -- a single dirt street, a few adobe 
N|               houses; a few racks of dried meat; a burro with a load of 
N|               faggots on its back being driven along by a small boy; the 
N|               music coming from a little cantina in the middle of the 
N|               street. Before the cantina is a bone rack of a horse, without 
N|               a saddle -- only a blanket pad. There is something familiar 
N|               about the horse and Ethan is staring at it as they ride in.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE CANTINA - FULL SHOT - DAY
O|
N|               The two men dismount, beating dust out of their clothes.
O|
N|               Ethan takes another look at the sorry old nag tied outside.
O|
N|               Martin pauses beside Ethan.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Recognize it?
O|
N|               Martin shakes his head. They start toward the cantina.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         There couldn't be two like that in 
U|                         all the world...
O|
S,G|               INT. THE CANTINA - FULL SHOT - DAY
O|
N|               as Ethan and Martin enter. It is a dirt-floored room with a 
N|               small bar near the door, chairs and benches along the walls. 
N,I|               A pair of guitar players are at the far end of the room. A 
N,I|               couple of Mexicans are playing a noisy game of dominoes, 
N,I|               slapping the dominoes down hard... EMILIO FIGUEROA, back to 
N|               the door, spurred boots across one of the tables, is sipping 
N,I|               a drink. Emilio is a cynical, middle-aged, aristocratic-
N,I|               looking man in modified charro costume. Watching the domino 
N,I|               game is ESTRELLA, lithe, sensuous, smoking a brown-paper 
N,I|               cigarette; she is barefoot. Behind the bar is the proprietor, 
N|               dozing on his stool. Ethan takes a step into the room.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (loudly)
U|                         MOSE!.... MOSE HARPER?
O|
N|               The proprietor awakens. The domino game is suspended in mid-
N|               play. Estrella turns... and from beside Emilio, previously 
N|               obscured by the man's back and the big charro hat, pokes the 
N|               head of old Mose. Emilio turns then to look at the newcomers.
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         Ay-eh...?
O|
N|               He is on his feet and advancing to meet them. As he recognizes 
N|               them a wide, foolish grin splits his face and his mouth opens 
N|               and closes in words that won't come out. He grabs and shakes 
N|               Ethan's arm, then Martin's.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Leggo my arm... You look mangier 'n 
U|                         ever.
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         Ain't been too good... No sir, not 
U|                         too good... Gettin' old, Ethan...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         You were born old...
O|
C|                                     PROPRIETOR
P|                              (all smiles - as they 
P|                              head for the bar)
U|                         Bienvenidos, senores... Pulque?...  
U|                         tequila?... mescal?... huiskey?
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Tequila...
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         Lo mismo.
O|
C|                                     PROPRIETOR
P|                              (beaming)
U|                         Y' par' el Viejo -- el vino del 
U|                         pais... tequila tambien!
O|
N|               Martin puts his back to the bar, leans elbows on it and looks 
N|               around.
O|
O|               158A
O|
S,G|               INT. THE CANTINA - ANGLING PAST MARTIN TOWARD ESTRELLA
O|
N|               She is giving him an appraising once-over, then signals the 
N|               musicians to play. She rests her buttocks against a table 
N|               and waits, her eyes challenging Martin to make a move. Emilio 
N|               is watching Estrella and Martin with something akin to bored 
N|               amusement.
O|
O|               158B
O|
S,G|               INT. THE CANTINA - ANGLE AT BAR
O|
N|               Ethan has poured a drink for Mose, now one for himself --
N|               leaving Martin's glass empty.
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         I been helpin' ye, Ethan... I been 
U|                         lookin' all the time...
O|
N|               Martin turns back to the bar to take his glass. He finds it 
N|               empty.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (to Ethan's back)
U|                         Thanks for nothin'...
O|
N|               He angrily throws a coin on the counter, appropriates the 
N|               bottle and his glass and heads for a table closer to Estrella. 
N|               Neither Ethan nor Mose seems aware of his going.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Well, the reward still stands...
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         Don't want no money, Ethan... jus' a 
U|                         place -- a roof over m' head... a 
U|                         little grub... a bunk to sleep in... 
U|                         an' a rockin' chair by the fire...  
U|                         my own rockin' chair by a fire...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         You help me find her, you got your 
U|                         rockin' chair...
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         Swear it, Ethan?... Given word?
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (impatiently)
U|                         Told ya, didn't I?
O|
C|                                     MOSE
P|                              (impressively)
U|                         Ethan... I found a man's seen her...  
U|                         knows where little Debbie is!
O|
N|               Ethan stares at him. Mose nods his reaffirmation of it.
O|
N|               Then Ethan's hand locks on the old man's shoulder.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Who? Where is he... this man?
O|
N|               Mose winces under the grip. He can't speak, but he looks 
N|               past Ethan and he points. Ethan turns. Emilio swings his 
N|               boots off the table and slowly crosses to them. He lets 
N|               cigarette smoke curl out of his mouth. Then he smiles.
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
U|                         I am this man, senor... Emilio Gabriel 
U|                         Fernandez y Figueroa... at your 
U|                         service...
P|                              (afterthought)
U|                         ...for a price...
P|                              (he smiles)
U|                         ...Always for a price...
O|
N|               As the men study each other, Estrella begins her dance --  
N|               and the rhythmic click of the castanets will beat like a 
N|               metronome. Emilio looks at the bottle on the bar, disdainfully 
N|               pushes it away and imperiously signals the proprietor to 
N|               bring something better.
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
U|                         Un otra!
O|
S,G|               INT. THE CANTINA - ANGLING PAST MARTIN TO ESTRELLA
O|
N|               He is knocking off his tequila and looking at the girl 
N|               hungrily. She is doing her swaying dance, playing up to him 
N|               and with unmistakable effect. He sloshes another drink into 
N|               his glass and, never taking his eyes off her, downs it.
O|
S,G|               INT. THE CANTINA - FULL SHOT - ANOTHER ANGLE - THE DANCE
O|
N,G|               Beyond Estrella we see Ethan, Emilio, and Mose at the bar --
N|               gestures, headshakes, the entire pantomime of an inaudible 
N|               conversation. Then Ethan takes out his pouch and begins 
N|               dropping gold pieces into Emilio's hand. Meanwhile, dance 
N|               and dancer are achieving their purpose with Martin. And the 
N|               tequila is working. He gets to his feet, a little groggily. 
N|               Estrella's smile deepens and there is a clear invitation in 
N|               her eyes... Ethan turns then, his deal with Emilio concluded, 
N|               and he sees what is going on. He starts for Martin's table 
N|               just as Martin moves out to take the girl.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (tolerantly)
U|                         Come on, Don Juan... We're on our 
U|                         way...
O|
N|               Martin tries to push him away, his eyes still on the girl.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (thickly)
U|                         Lemme alone...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (taking his arm)
U|                         You breeds are all alike -- two drinks 
U|                         an'...
O|
N|               Martin breaks free and squares off.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         Take yer hands off'n me... This lady 
U|                         an' me got some things to talk over!
O|
N|               Estrella ranges herself alongside of Martin and slips an arm 
N|               possessively through his.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (a shrug)
U|                         Suit yourself... While you're enjoyin' 
U|                         your little conversation, I'll be 
U|                         ridin' out with Senor Fernandez 
U|                         here...  The Comanch' medicine country 
U|                         ain't far... there's one camp with a 
U|                         chief named Cicatriz.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         Never heard of him...
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Cicatriz is Mex for Scar... an' he 
U|                         has a white girl in his tepee... Be 
U|                         seein' you...
O|
N|               He turns and heads out. Emilio, who has come up behind him, 
N|               gives Estrella a slight smile and bow.
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
U|                         Buena suerte, Estrella... Hasta la 
U|                         vista.
O|
N|               He follows Ethan. Estrella swings her body close to Martin 
N|               and lets her arms slide around his neck.
O|
C|                                     ESTRELLA
P|                              (softly)
U|                         Tu quieres...?
O|
N|               Martin blinks to clear away the fog of tequila and desire.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (a bitter laugh)
U|                         Sure... sure... Only not this year...
O|
N|               He pulls her arms away and goes lurching after the others.
O|
N|               Mose catches his arm.
O|
C|                                     MOSE
U|                         'Mind Ethan 'bout my rockin' chair!
O|
N|               Martin continues out and Mose stands there -- his head rockin' 
N|               as though he already were in his chair.
O|
