O|
S|               EXT. (APPROACH TO THE NEEDLES MONUMENT) - A BROAD CANYON -
S,G|               FULL SHOT - DAY
O|
N,I|               A small pack train (nine or ten horses, six or seven men) 
N|               winds through a canyon behind which we can see huge needles 
N|               of rock: majestic, savage country. At the head of the column 
N|               ride Ethan, Emilio, and Martin. Behind them come Emilio's 
N|               cargadores -- lean, hard-bitten wiry little Mexicans. One 
N,I|               leads a handsome palomino. The SOUND of the CASTANETS ECHOES 
N|               in the musical theme.
O|
S,G|               EXT. CANYON - MED. SHOT - HEAD OF COLUMN - MOVING - DAY
O|
N|               Ethan is looking around with grim interest.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         Medicine country, huh?
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
P|                              (slight smile)
U|                         Medicine so strong they believe the 
U|                         feather of an eagle found here can 
U|                         guard a man against bullets...
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (looking ahead)
U|                         If you got one handy, now's the 
U|                         time...
O|
N|               Ethan and Emilio both look in the direction of his glance.
O|
S,G|               EXT. CANYON - FULL SHOT - REVERSE ANGLE - FROM BEHIND RIDERS - 
S|               DAY
O|
N,G|               They are turning a bend and now, ahead, we see a cordon of 
N|               Comanches -- all armed, all quiet, all very menacing as they 
N|               watch the approaching column. Emilio calls a greeting in 
N|               Comanche. It gets no answer.
O|
S,G|               EXT. CANYON - MOVING SHOT - THE COMANCHE FACES - DAY
O|
N,G|               The CAMERA PICKS UP the faces in turn, as from the white 
N,G|               men's viewpoint, as they ride slowly by.
O|
S,G|               EXT. COMANCHE ENCAMPMENT - FULL SHOT - DAY
O|
N|               One tepee stands apart from the other's -- Scar's. Beyond it 
N|               are other tepees, the gathering of braves and some squaws, 
N|               the drying racks for meat, etc. Emilio leads the way toward 
N|               the central tepee. They dismount nearby and Emilio inclines 
N|               his head toward the one tepee. Ethan and Martin brace 
N|               themselves and wait. The flap of the tepee is closed.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (gruffly)
U|                         What are we waiting for?
O|
N|               Emilio cautions him with a hand gesture.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE TEPEE - MED. CLOSE SHOT - DAY
O|
N|               The flap is thrust aside and SCAR steps out -- the same 
N|               Comanche we had seen at the grave when Debbie was captured.
O|
N|               He stands tall, arrogant, eyeing the white men with hard, 
N|               implacable eyes. He has a robe gathered about him. Across 
N|               his face is a scar.
O|
C,E|                                     EMILIO'S VOICE
U|                         Senores! This is Cicatriz!
O|
S,G|               EXT. INDIAN ENCAMPMENT - CLOSE SHOT - ETHAN AND MARTIN - DAY
O|
N|               The white men's eyes are as hard as the Comanche's: this is 
N|               the man they have long sought, the killer, the raper.
O|
N|               They cannot mask the hatred they feel.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (slowly... at last)
U|                         Scar... It's plain to see where you 
U|                         got your name.
O|
N|               Scar's hand moves to the scar on his face, and a finger runs 
N|               along it.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
U|                         Your name - Big Shoulders... His 
U|                         name - He Who Follows.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         You speak pretty good American for a 
U|                         Comanche... Someone teach you?
O|
N|               It is a leading question and Scar knows it. He looks long at 
N|               Ethan and the suggestion of a smile touches his lips.
O|
N|               But he makes no direct answer. He looks instead to Emilio.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
P|                              (in Comanche)
U|                         Ah-we pabbo-tie-bo ee-kee-tay?
U|                              (Why did you bring 
U|                              the gringos here?)
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
P|                              (a shrug -- in Comanche)
U|                         Pabbo-tie-bo kim te-moo-er.
U|                              (The gringos want to 
U|                              trade.)
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
U|                         That's right... We come to trade... 
U|                         Only not out here...
P|                              (with sign language 
P|                              accompaniment)
U|                         I don't stand talkin' in the wind.
O|
N|               Emilio quickly turns and calls to one of his men. Emilio is 
N|               worried.
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
P|                              (sharply)
U|                         Miguel... caballo -- aca!
O|
N,I|               One of the Mexicans comes on the trot, leading in the 
N|               palomino. Emilio makes a gesture -- giving it to Scar.
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
U|                         Co-bay tabitz-chat.
P|                              (meaning)
U|                              (Very fine horse.)
O|
N|               Scar looks at it greedily, then nods. He'll accept it.
O|
N|               He looks at Ethan. Again that faintly contemptuous smile.
O|
N|               He signals them to enter his tepee.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (to Martin)
U|                         Stay out here.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         Not likely.
O|
N|               He follows Ethan into the tepee... and a worried Emilio goes 
N|               along.
O|
S,G|               INT. THE TEPEE - FULL SHOT
O|
N|               A small fire burns in the center of the lodge and a shaft of 
N|               sunlight strikes in from the smoke flap at the peak.
O|
N|               Two chunky squaws, who have been tending the fire or grinding 
N|               corn in a rock pestle, scuttle to a side of the tepee. Two 
N,I|               others, one half-grown and the other slightly taller, sit 
N|               with their backs to the fire, huddled over some leather work 
N|               or stitching. Both are shawled. As Scar enters, he barks a 
N|               word to the squaws near the fire.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
U|                         Pie-kay! (Clear out!)
P|                              (then he turns to the 
P|                              white men)
U|                         IH-CARD!
U|                              (Sit!)
O|
N|               He sits on some robes, signs for them to sit opposite.
O|
N|               Slowly they look around them.
O|
S,G|               INT. THE TEPEE - REVERSE ANGLE - AS FROM THEIR VIEWPOINT -
S|               TTE TWO OLDER SQUAWS
O|
N|               They are sitting with heads averted, slightly profiled -- 
N|               but clearly Indian women, broad-faced, dark of hair and skin.
O|
C,E|                                     EMILIO'S VOICE
U|                         His sons are dead... So his wives 
U|                         sit on the honor side of his lodge.
O|
S,G|               INT. THE TEPEE - ANGLING PAST ETHAN TOWARD THE TWO YOUNG 
S|               ONES
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (glancing at them)
U|                         Are those his wives too?
O|
N,I,G|               One of the squaws turns -- and even in the shadows we see it 
N|               is another Indian face. The other does not turn.
O|
N|               Scar leans in, blocking the view.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
U|                         Two sons -- killed by white men... 
U|                         For each son, I take many scalps...
P|                              (in Comanche)
U|                         Mayah-kay zee-eh!...
U|                              (Bring the lance!)
O|
N|               The slightly smaller of the young squaws stiffens but doesn't 
N|               move. Scar glares.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
P|                              (louder)
U|                         MAYAH-KAY ZEE-EH!
O|
N|               The girl gets to her feet. Ethan and Martin watch as, still 
N|               with averted face, she crosses to where a lance hangs from 
N|               the tepee wall. It has several scalps on it, including one 
N|               with light red hair. Slowly she carries it back. Scar never 
N|               takes his eyes from the faces of the white men, savoring 
N|               every moment of it. The girl extends the lance between them, 
N|               so that it is like a bare blade separating two duelists. 
N|               Neither Ethan nor Martin dares at first look at more than 
N|               the scalp pole... Then slowly their eyes lift... and the 
N,G|               CAMERA MOVES IN and RAISES TO:
O|
S,G|               INT. THE TEPEE - EXTREME CLOSE SHOT - DEBBIE
O|
N|               As the shawl slips back to reveal her light hair, the slant 
N|               green eyes looking at them from a tanned, but still white 
N,M|               and very beautiful face. (NOTE TO MUSIC: The SEARCH THEME 
M|               should cover all the foregoing action -- but at the first 
M|               clear view of DEBBIE, it ends dramatically.)
O|
N|               Debbie's eyes hold theirs -- and then Scar's voice is heard:
O|
C|                                     SCAR
P|                              (in Comanche)
U|                         Pie-kay!
U|                              (Go!)
O|
N|               Swiftly she straightens, takes away the scalp pole and goes 
N|               back to her former place.
O|
S,G|               INT. THE TEPEE - MED. CLOSE SHOT - THE GROUP AS BEFORE
O|
N|               Scar is watching the white men like a hawk. Martin's eyes 
N|               are wide and his breathing seems suspended. But Ethan is 
N|               playing it like a poker player.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (his voice controlled)
U|                         I've seen scalps before...
O|
N|               Scar's eyes are mocking. He lets his robe slip back from his 
N|               shoulders, revealing a bare bronzed chest on which -- glinting 
N|               in the reflected firelight -- is the medallion that Ethan 
N|               had given Debbie. It is suspended by a chain or rawhide 
N|               string. Scar touches it.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
U|                         This before?
O|
N|               Ethan smiles -- and he's still playing poker. He stands...  
N|               and the others follow. Scar is puzzled.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (to Emilio)
U|                         I came to trade, not to admire his 
U|                         collection... Tell him we're going 
U|                         to pitch camp across the crick... 
U|                         Maybe we can talk trade tomorrow.
O|
N|               Scar hasn't understood all of it. He scowls and looks at 
N|               Emilio.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
P|                              (in Comanche)
U|                         Ee-sap! Pabbo-tie-bo ee-sap!
U|                              (He lies! The gringo 
U|                              lies!)
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
P|                              (placatingly)
U|                         Tomorrow -- manana -- 'puetze.'
O|
N|               Scar looks at Ethan and at Martin. He smiles slightly, and 
N|               he nods his agreement.
O|
C|                                     SCAR
U|                         Puetze!
O|
N|               Martin and Ethan turn to go. Only then does Debbie look 
N|               swiftly at them and as swiftly away. Martin can't help but 
N|               pause, but Ethan prods him toward the tepee flap.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE TEPEE - MED. CLOSE SHOT - MOVING - THE THREE MEN
O|
N|               They emerge from the tepee and begin to walk away -- not 
N|               fast, not slow, and without a side glance. Scar emerges and 
N|               looks after them -- glowering. A few other Comanches begin 
N|               to gather near his tepee.
O|
C|                                     EMILIO
P|                              (urgently)
U|                         Walk with dignity!
P|                              (after a moment; lips 
P|                              hardly moving)
U|                         If you gringo heretics have any 
U|                         prayers, say them...
O|
