O|
N,I|               He stands with another Comanche -- inspecting the horse lines. 
N|               In the near distance the dog's barking is heard -- shrill, 
N|               insistent. Scar, almost like an animal, sniffs the air 
N|               suspiciously. The other Comanche laughs.
O|
C|                                     COMANCHE
U|                         Tahbo.
U|                              (A rabbit.)
O|
N|               Scar isn't satisfied but returns to his work.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE COMANCHE CAMP - ANGLE FAVORING SCAR'S TEPEE PAST 
S|               MARTIN
O|
N|               He is staring at it -- remembering it from certain distinct 
N|               decorations. The lower part of the tepee is rolled up, for 
N|               better air circulation. Martin wraps Charlie's blanket around 
N|               him and begins walking to the tepee.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE LEE OF THE HOGBACK OR RISE - FULL SHOT - THE 
S|               RANGERS
O|
N|               The line is drawn up, dismounted, the men checking their 
N|               sidearms, tightening cinches, etc. Sam moves briskly along 
N|               the line of men, then mounts his horse.
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
U|                         The State of Texas is payin' you 
U|                         boys $12 a month. Here's your chance 
U|                         to earn it... Now I don't want any 
U|                         foolin' around after scalps. We ain't 
U|                         got the time... Yankee cavalry's on 
U|                         its way here to set those Comanches 
U|                         free... We gotta beat 'em to it -- 
U|                         our way... Now mount an' guide center 
U|                         on young Greenhill here. Son, you 
U|                         just follow me... And WATCH THAT 
U|                         KNIFE!
O|
N|               The last comes as Greenhill tries to mount, simultaneously 
N|               flailing out his sabre perilously close to Clayton.
O|
S,G|               EXT. SCAR'S TEPEE - MED. CLOSE SHOT - MARTIN
O|
N|               Martin has gained the tepee, takes one quick look around, 
N|               then strides in.
O|
S|               INT. SCAR'S TEPEE
O|
N|               as Martin enters. One robe, tossed back, shows where Scar 
N|               has slept. Against one wall lie the huddled figures of two 
N,I|               squaws. Two other figures -- one being Debbie's -- sleep in 
N|               robes. Martin's eyes go to Scar's place and then rove slowly 
N|               and hold on:
O|
S,G|               INT. TEPEE - MED. CLOSE SHOT - DEBBIE
O|
N|               She moves slightly so that the light strikes her fair hair. 
N|               Martin kneels close to her and gently touches her shoulder, 
N|               to awaken her. Her eyes open, then widen in fear. Martin 
N|               quickly claps a hand over his mouth.
O|
N|               Silently she fights him, trying to pull the hand away.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (a hoarse whisper)
U|                         Debbie... don't!
O|
N|               And then her fingernails rake his hand. He winces and lets 
N|               go. She screams and tries to leap up. He grabs her. The other 
N|               squaws wake -- begin squawking.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
P|                              (grimly)
U|                         I'm takin' you whether you want to 
U|                         or not...
O|
N|               Debbie sees something beyond him. Her eyes widen.
O|
C|                                     DEBBIE
P|                              (a scream)
U|                         MARTIN!
O|
S,G|               INT. TEPEE - SHOOTING FROM EXTREME LOW ANGLE, AS FROM HER 
S,G|               P.O.V. - SCAR
O|
N|               He looms in the flap of the tepee much as he had over the 
N|               grave when he first kidnapped her. He has a scalping knife 
N,G|               in his hand. Scar whips his arm back. Behind him we see the 
N,G|               other Comanche -- the one from the horse herd.
O|
S,G|               INT. TEPEE - FULL SHOT
O|
N|               Martin spins and his gun is out. He fans the trigger hammer 
N|               twice -- and both slugs hit Scar. As the other Comanche leaps 
N|               in, Martin's gun speaks again. And then he is on his feet, 
N|               catching Debbie by the arm -- unresisting now -- and pulling 
N|               her after him through the camp. Shouts and cries bespeak the 
N|               awakening of the camp.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE HOGBACK - FULL SHOT - THE RANGER GROUP
O|
N|               They are mounted and in line. The echo of a shot comes 
N|               bouncing back. Clayton is facing the men. He solemnly doffs 
N|               his hat and bows his head.
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
U|                         For these Thy gifts which we are 
U|                         about to receive...
P|                              (donning his hat, he 
P|                              turns to the bugler)
U|                         Sound that horn, son, and Leave Us 
U|                         Go Amongst Them...YA-HEE!
O|
N|               And shouting the rebel yell, he leads the charge. The yell 
N|               is picked up by every man and the bugler sounds the charge 
N,I|               as though he had a regiment at his back. Lt. Greenhill -- a 
N,I|               West Point beau sabreur in strange company -- has his sabre 
N|               at the "charge."
O|
G|               FULL MOVING SHOT - ON THE CHARGE
O|
N,G|               In the swamp of men and horses, we just have time to notice 
N,G|               that Sam is darting a somewhat worried look at Greenhill's 
N|               saber ominously close to his back. The charge is sounding, 
N|               the hooves are drumming and the men are yelling the way they 
N|               did when they rode with Bedford Forrest -- reins in their 
N|               teeth, guns in their fists.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE ENCAMPMENT - FULL SHOT - AS THE RANGERS HIT
O|
N|               Comanches are running from the tepees, trying to reach their 
N|               horse herd as the file of Rangers knifes in.  The bark of 
N|               hand guns is a steady sound now -- and the Comanches have 
N,I|               never been up against such marksmen. One charging Ranger 
N,I|               rides down a tepee. Another, with two guns drawn, scores a 
N|               running double on two Indians racing toward him from opposite 
N|               sides of his fast-running horse. A dodging Comanche screams 
N|               as a barrel-chested roan hits him broadside and sends him 
N|               sprawling into the embers of the campfire.
O|
S,G|               EXT. SCAR'S TEPEE - FULL SHOT - ETHAN
O|
N|               He yanks his horse back to its haunches, firing at a Comanche 
N|               crouched near the tepee. The charge races past him. Ethan 
N|               swings off and runs to the tepee flap, whips it open and 
N|               glares. A squaw comes running out at him, knife upraised, 
N|               but he knocks her sprawling with a full arm sweep. He turns 
N|               and his face is a mask of frustration -- and then he freezes, 
N|               seeing what he has been looking for:
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE INDIAN ENCAMPMENT - LONG SHOT - ETHAN'S P.O.V.
O|
N|               Martin is running with Debbie, trying to pull her along, 
N|               while she kicks and claws and tries to break free. (They are 
N|               about thirty yards from the camp proper.) Riderless horses 
N|               are milling, circling. O.s. we can hear the firing, the yells.
O|
S,G|               EXT. SCAR'S TEPEE - FULL SHOT
O|
N|               Ethan remounts and heads around the side of Scar's tepee.
O|
N|               In the distance we see Martin and Debbie. The Rangers, 
N|               regrouping, are charging back through the camp, driving the 
N|               Comanche horse herd.
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - MED. CLOSE SHOT - MARTIN AND DEBBIE
O|
N|               Martin hears Ethan's horse riding down at them; he turns, 
N|               and Debbie pulls free and starts to run away.
O|
C|                                     MARTIN
U|                         No, Ethan! NO!
O|
N|               He goes running into the path of Ethan's horse.
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - MOVING SHOT - ETHAN
O|
N|               as Martin runs and grabs hold of his stirrup, trying to fight 
N|               the horse to a stop. Ethan swings on him -- once, twice -- 
N|               and Martin is knocked sprawling. Ethan rides on, relentlessly.
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - FULL SHOT - DEBBIE AND ETHAN
O|
N|               She is running and dodging, trying to escape the horseman.
O|
N|               Ethan has his gun drawn. She ducks to one side and the horse 
N|               goes past. Both figures are almost obscured in the dust. 
N|               Ethan spins his mount and charges after her. She runs and 
N|               then falls -- and he is off his horse and striding toward 
N|               her.
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - ETHAN AND DEBBIE
O|
N,G|               Ethan is at the left of CAMERA and slightly closer to the 
N,G|               foreground, with Debbie at the right, supine on the ground 
N|               and the dust swirling around her. Ethan draws and raises his 
N|               gun. The hammer goes back.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (quietly)
U|                         I'm sorry, girl... Shut your eyes...
O|
N,G|               The dust clears. The CAMERA MOVES slightly forward along the 
N,G|               gun arm and HOLDS on Debbie's face -- the eyes gazing 
N,G|               fearlessly, innocently into Ethan's. We HOLD for a long moment 
N|               and then the gun lowers. Ethan slowly holsters it and walks 
N|               over to her.
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - CLOSE SHOT - ETHAN
O|
N|               He looks down at her.
O|
C|                                     ETHAN
P|                              (softly)
U|                         You sure favor your mother...
O|
S,G|               EXT. OPEN COUNTRY - MED. CLOSE SHOT - THE TWO
O|
N|               He extends his hand to her. She takes it and he helps her to 
N|               her feet. And then she is against his chest and his arm goes 
N|               protectingly about her. They are standing that way when Martin 
N|               stumbles up -- and stares.
O|
T|                                                                   WIPE TO:
O|
O|               233-A
O|
S,G|               EXT. COMANCHE CAMP - WIDE ANGLE - DAY
O|
N|               The line of Rangers is afoot now, each man near his horse, 
N|               each man with rifle out, pumping shot after shot at the 
N|               fleeting remnant of Comanches riding down the long valley 
N|               with their scattered horse herd milling and criss-crossing 
N|               in mid-ground... And then as the firing slackens, from afar 
N|               we can hear the blare of a cavalry bugle sounding the charge: 
N|               sign of the approach of Greenhill's troop.
O|
T|                                                               DISSOLVE TO:
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE INDIAN ENCAMPMENT - FULL SHOT - FAVORING CLAYTON
O|
N|               It is perhaps half an hour later. Clayton is very grim of 
N|               face and occasionally wincing. His trousers are down over 
N|               his ankles, his shirt-tails flapping over his long-handled 
N|               red underwear. Behind him Charlie MacCorry is kneeling, 
N|               applying some crude first aid to Sam's rump. Beyond them 
N|               some of the Rangers are readying for the move-out. Sam looks 
N,I|               up angrily as COLONEL GREENHILL, a guidon bearer, and bugler 
N|               ride in. Greenhill is a choleric man.
O|
C|                                     COLONEL
U|                         Clayton, if you were in my command 
U|                         I'd have you courtmartialed for this!
O|
C|                                     CLAYTON
P|                              (angrily, to MacCorry)
U|                         Hurry it up!
O|
C|                                     COLONEL
U|                         What's the matter, sir... You wounded?  
U|                         What is it, Sergeant -- a bullet or 
U|                         an arrow?
O|
N|               Charlie just gapes, but Clayton -- grim-lipped -- bends a 
N|               meaningful glare on someone off. Greenhill looks that way.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE INDIAN ENCAMPMENT - CLOSE SHOT - LT. GREENHILL
O|
N|               He is standing with his sabre at salute -- looking as 
N|               miserable as any shavetail would look when in disgrace. It 
N|               could be that the end of the sabre has a pronounced bend.
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE INDIAN ENCAMPMENT - FULL SHOT - THE GROUP
O|
N|               Sam suddenly slaps MacCorry's hand away and grabs for his 
N|               pants and pulls them up -- and around a tepee come three 
N|               figures -- Ethan, Debbie, and Martin -- with Ethan holding 
N|               Debbie's hand. And Sam's face is split by a grin as he tucks 
N|               his shirt-tails in and goes to meet them.
O|
T|                                                               DISSOLVE TO:
O|
S,G|               EXT. THE JORGENSEN HOUSE - CLOSE SHOT - MOSE HARPER -
S|               AFTERNOON
O|
N|               He is in a rocker on the porch. Suddenly he stares out and 
N|               stops rocking. Faint in the distance, but coming closer, the 
N|               clop-clop of two horses, moving at a walk. A moment later 
N|               Jorgensen emerges and comes to stand beside Mose, shading 
N|               his eyes and squinting against the sun, still not recognizing 
N|               the distant horsemen.  He is joined by Mrs. Jorgensen. And 
N|               then Laurie comes out and she too stares, frowning at first, 
N|               then with dawning realization.
O|
N|               Lars and Mrs. Jorgensen also begin to guess... to suspect...  
N|               and then to know. And Laurie starts to run.
O|
G|               CLOSE SHOT - MOVING WITH LAURIE
O|
N,G|               The CAMERA MOVES ahead of her as she runs blindly over the 
N|               hard-packed ground, running as hard as she can toward the 
N|               still unseen but nearing horsemen.
O|
G|               FULL SHOT - THE GROUP
O|
N|               Ethan has Debbie on the pommel of his saddle, his arm 
N|               supporting her, and she is asleep. Martin is riding beside 
N|               them. Laurie comes running up to stare at Ethan and at the 
N|               girl. He smiles and puts a finger to his lips -- cautioning 
N|               her against waking Debbie -- and then he rides by. Laurie 
N|               looks then at Martin. He doesn't know whether to smile or 
N|               not; he just waits. And then she is beside him and she steps 
N|               onto his stirruped foot and vaults up beside him, and she 
N|               kisses him just as she had on the day he left the graves to 
N|               take up the search. And still holding her beside him, he 
N|               rides slowly after Ethan and Debbie toward the house.
O|
T|                                                                   FADE OUT
O|
M|                                         THE END
O|

